---
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import QuestAtlas from "../../components/clubhouse/QuestAtlas";
import { BUILDINGS } from "../../lib/atlas";

export const prerender = true;

export const title = "Quest Atlas";
export const navCategory = "clubhouse";
---

<Layout title="Quest Atlas - The Clubhouse, Matrix Hub">
  <div class="container mx-auto px-4 py-8">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-4 text-green-400">🗺️ Quest Atlas</h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 mb-4">
      The Clubhouse's front door is a place, not a menu. This is Matrix Hub
      drawn as a small town — the Proving Ground, the Casino, the
      Clubhouse rooms, the Studio, the Academy — and you walk it. Step up
      to any door and it opens onto the real page.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6">
      Walk with <strong class="text-gray-100">WASD or the arrow keys</strong>,
      tap or click anywhere on the map to walk there, or use the on-screen
      pad on a phone. The twin fountains and the gold MTX coin mark the
      plaza at the centre of town. To see what a building is before you
      walk over: hover it with a pointer, tab to its door marker, or press
      and hold it on a touch screen — a preview card names it first.
    </p>

    <div
      class="bg-green-900/40 border-2 border-green-500 rounded-lg p-4 mb-8 text-green-100"
    >
      <strong>Nothing here is trapped in the game:</strong> every building
      on the map is also listed as a plain link below the map, and every
      door leads to a real, live page. The map itself is drawn entirely in
      code — no downloaded sprites, no external assets.
    </div>

    <QuestAtlas client:only="react" />

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4 text-green-400">
      Every door, as a link
    </h2>
    <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {
        BUILDINGS.map((b) => (
          <a
            href={b.href}
            class="block rounded-lg border border-green-700/60 bg-gray-900/60 p-4 hover:border-amber-400 hover:bg-gray-900 transition-colors"
          >
            <span class="block text-lg font-bold text-amber-300">{b.name}</span>
            <span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-gray-300">{b.blurb}</span>
            <span class="mt-3 block text-sm font-semibold text-green-400">
              {b.enterLabel} →
            </span>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <p class="mt-10 text-sm text-gray-500">
      Built from the commons: the Atlas runs on the site's own stack (Astro,
      React, Tailwind CSS — MIT) with a hand-rolled tile engine, so the map
      ships as one small script instead of a game-engine download. The Quest
      Atlas concept comes from the Games division's Genius Strike board
      (melonJS, MIT, was the reference for the Tiled-style map layout).
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
